{
 "id": "gripper-bracket-creep-fdm",
 "brief": "Bracket between my arm's wrist-roll flange and the gripper, PETG on an FDM printer, printed on its\nside with layers along +Z. The flange is a vertical face at x = 0 (the roll axis is X); the bracket\nhas a 4 mm mounting plate on it (x = 0 to 4, y and z = -15 to 15) held by four M3 socket screws\nalong X at (y, z) = (+/-12, +/-12) through 3.4 mm holes, heads on the plate's x = 4 face, and each\nneeds a 7 mm diameter clear column along +X right out to the end of the part for the hex key. The\ngripper cable runs along the roll axis: keep a 10 mm diameter hole clear along X through the whole\npart. At the far end the gripper bolts onto a 4 mm pad (x = 56 to 60, y = -15 to 15, z = -8 to 8)\nwith two M3 screws into heat-set inserts at (y, z) = (+/-10, 0): 4 mm holes, 6 mm deep from the x =\n60 face. The part fits in x = 0 to 60, y and z = -18 to 18. The gripper with what it carries is 0.9\nkg with its centre of mass 40 mm beyond the pad (x = 100) on the axis, and gravity is -Z. The arm\nparks holding it like that for most of the day: about 20,000 hours over its life, inside an\nenclosure that sits at 55 C. Moving, it sees the same 0.9 kg at 2 g, and the roll servo can stall at\n0.6 N·m about X with the gripper jammed. Safety factor 2 on the moving and stall loads, and 2\nagainst creep rupture under the parked load at 55 C. Under 20 g.",
 "attachments": [],
